5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,280
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard.

7
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:49,360
Today's conversation is shorter than usual and will be featured in RevenueCat's State of Subscription Apps report.

8
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:57,040
Each episode in this series will explore one crucial topic and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

9
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:01,840
With me today, Anmel Tavari, Director of Product at Duolingo.

10
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:18,000
On the podcast, I talk with Anmel about how Duolingo prioritizes clarity over persuasion on their paywalls, why they offer users multiple free trials instead of just one, and how adding friction to their trial reminder flow actually boosted conversions.

11
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:21,520
Hey, Ann Mull, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

12
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:22,160
Hi, David.

13
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:23,680
Yeah, really excited to be here.

14
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:24,640
Thank you for having me.

15
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:36,080
So I had your Chief Product Officer, Jim on the podcast recently and was so fascinated talking to him about the free trial dynamics and even having a free trial for a freemium app.

16
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:39,920
So folks can go back and listen to that episode if they want to dig into some of those lessons.

17
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:42,240
But there was so much we didn't have time to talk about.

18
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:50,640
And so I thought it would be fun to have you on to kind of dig deeper into some of the deeper lessons that Duolingo's learned about freemium and free trials.

19
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:52,720
So I wanted to start at a really high level.

20
00:01:53,440 --> 00:02:02,400
What would you say is kind of the guiding principle for Duolingo around fre trials and getting people to experience the premium features?

21
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:03,120
Yeah.

22
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:17,360
I think, you know, the first thing that comes to mind is when you're trying to sell a product, it's incredibly hard to tell you or even describe to a user what the value proposition of that product is.

23
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:30,080
Now, we can do it through kind of like UX or like certain copy or even an ad, but the best way for a user to really know what you get in a subscription is by trying it.

24
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:36,240
And we've seen it as such an effective lever that when you're trying to explain that you get unlimited energy.

25
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:40,880
And so, if you have limited energy or no ads, the best way to kind of experience that is by trying it.

26
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:42,880
You're not going to understand the benefit.

27
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:50,960
And so, for us, trials are a really effective lever to try and get people to adopt our paid features.

28
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:55,360
And then how do you position that in a way that makes it clear?

29
00:02:55,920 --> 00:03:01,360
One of the things that we've learned is our goal is not to like persuade users to try this.

30
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:05,040
Our goal is to give them clarity and confidence to purchase.

31
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:14,240
So, you know, the most effective way for users to be able to feel confident to try a trial is to know, like, well, what does that really entail?

32
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:20,240
That is anything from being able to explain in a timeline what happens on which day of the trial.

33
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:29,600
We've seen even if, for example, when you're on one plan and you want to move to a different plan, trying to be clear about, like, what, what is, how much money are you going to get back?

34
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:32,160
The refunds clarity is so important.

35
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:46,480
And, you know, very recently, a great example of this is that we've had this functionality where when you sign up for a free trial, you'll get a reminder, a notification sent to you on, you know, about two days before your trial expires.

36
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:52,480
And we decided, we decided to test, well, what about, what if we let you choose which day you get that reminder?

37
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,160
And counterintuitive like that.

38
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:57,920
We could have, like, that normally you would think that friction is probably not a good thing.

39
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:06,080
But what we saw was that, you know, that drew attention to the fact that you can choose when you want to do a reminder and we're going to remind you.

40
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:07,760
And so you can decide to cancel later.

41
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:15,360
That buys us enough time so that you wait till that fifth day or sixth day to decide really, okay, am I getting value out of this?

42
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:21,680
And it was a really great way to increase confidence in what you're in the product that you're going to try.

43
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:24,800
Any other lessons around clarity in copy?

44
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:28,720
I know Duolingo has done a lot with multi step paywalls.

45
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,960
It seems like another way to introduce clarity.

46
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:32,320
Any other lessons there?

47
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:38,160
One of the things that I think users, of course, also care about is like not losing their progress.

48
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:52,160
And so what we've seen is that users are just much more likely to start a trial when the decision or like when we position it as, well, you know, if you've run out of hearts or energy, if you don't continue, you're going to basically lose your progress.

49
00:04:52,480 --> 00:05:07,760
And I think we've done it in a way that's like balances, kind of showing still some sense of progress, but trying to basically also indicate that, well, if you pay gems or if you try start this free trial now, that's going to basically you'll be able to kind of maintain your progress and finish this lesson.

50
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:12,320
I think the other lesson, the other kind of big lesson that we have seen is that there isn't.

51
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:14,160
is that there is no one size fits all.

52
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:17,920
A really great example of that is new users versus current users.

53
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:24,400
We know that when a user is brand new to Dualingo, they're actually much more likely to convert.

54
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:40,000
And what we've actually seen is that instead of trying to just like, you know, take our standard upsells that try to promote Super Dualingo and just put them in the onboarding flow, we found that it's much better to actually make it contextual to the onboarding.

55
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:44,400
So as an example, we asked like, how do you want to learn or how do you want to achieve your goals?

56
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:46,960
Is it with ads or without ads?

57
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:51,360
Or do you want to learn faster or do you want to just learn at the pace that you prefer?

58
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:51,520
or you would prefer.

59
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:59,200
And what we saw was that when that is kind of positioned as part of onboarding, we saw significantly higher kind of uptick rate.

60
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:00,960
It was one of our biggest wins last year.

61
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:15,360
And what it's telling us is that we can't while we some standardization is really helpful, especially if you think about it like, you know, our purchase flow, sometimes you need to be aware of kind of who the user is and when, you know, what, where they are in their life cycle.

62
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:15,840
Yeah.

63
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:17,280
That makes a lot of sense.

64
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:19,120
And what about trial duration?

65
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:25,040
Have you all experimented with like a month long free trial, three day free trial, all the way up and down the stack?

66
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:27,360
Or where do you land on duration of trial?

67
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:34,160
We used to have a two week trial., I think, towards the end of 2024.

68
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:45,760
And one of the things that we ended up doing was we tested a shorter duration, seven days, and we tested this with a couple of different combinations, but users are usually eligible for multiple trials.

69
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:50,640
And when we tested reducing it, we actually saw like two different benefits.

70
00:06:51,040 --> 00:07:00,160
The first was, of course, overall net, we saw more conversions over time in combination with like playing around with how many trials we can offer to a user.

71
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:07,600
But the second much more valuable benefit was when you start a trial and you have to wait 14 days for a user to convert.

72
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:11,600
But from an experimentation perspective, you have to wait 14 days.

73
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:19,920
And so we actually basically doubled our experimentation velocity because now instead of having to wait 14 days, you just get your results in seven days.

74
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:25,040
And imagine that compound over an entire quarter or over six months or over a year.

75
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:32,160
It was an incredibly powerful lever because we can start, we can know very quickly whether an experiment is going to perform well or not.

76
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,160
And then if it's not performing, that's great.

77
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:37,440
We can actually decide, okay, we can shut that down and move on to the next one.

78
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:40,320
So I think that has been an incredibly effective lever for us.

79
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:42,960
You were talking about multiple trials.

80
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:44,960
Tell me more about how that works.

81
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,920
And especially in light of tactically, you know, Apple.

82
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:54,160
on any one SKU will only allow the intro free offer to be eligible one time.

83
00:07:54,720 --> 00:08:00,400
So then you probably have some way of resetting that or sending them a different SKU or something like that.

84
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:04,320
So it's not it's not been typical and Apple doesn't really make it easy either.

85
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:07,760
Why offer multiple trials to users?

86
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:26,480
What we've realized is, I mean, as with anything, it's ultimately a question of like, what's going to be the easiest for a user to both basically feel confident in trying something out, but also we've also quickly realized that users are not always in the same life cycle stage.

87
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:30,960
And so there are advantages in having multiple trials for us.

88
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:41,680
The first is it's effective for us because we can try to understand, well, okay, maybe in the first time you didn't fully get to understand the benefits or maybe you tried it and you weren't sure.

89
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:47,120
Or you tried it and you were like super busy that one week and didn't actually get to experience it.

90
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:47,760
Exactly.

91
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:54,320
And maybe a simple framing of all this is that imagine if I asked you as from a business point of view, right?

92
00:08:54,560 --> 00:09:02,480
Or even from a user's point of view, you might benefit from having trying a product over a long, like over multiple periods, but spread across a little bit.

93
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:06,000
And from a business point of view, it's better because now you as a user.

94
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:11,040
have to decide four times, three or four times, whether you want to make this purchase or not.

95
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:15,760
And of course, you know, aggregated across those, we would see that it's an effective lever.

96
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:31,200
The second point that I want to make is what we've seen is that, of course, not everyone is as comfortable to start a trial or for whatever reason there's a lot of hesitation despite all the things that we try and do to try and increase clarity of when you're going to get a reminder, et cetera.

97
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:42,160
So we have this concept of a free taste and where we basically can offer you a trial like experience, which is three days but with no strings attached.

98
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:43,840
You get three days of the service.

99
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:49,520
And you end up, you can try the, try the subscription tier, super or max.

100
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:53,840
And at the end of the day at the end of that period, you get to decide whether you want to convert or not.

101
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:56,400
Or during the period, you can decide whether you want to convert or not.

102
00:09:56,560 --> 00:10:01,520
And that's been a really effective lever for not just free to pay, but paid to pay.

103
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:05,280
Because to your point about Apple, it's a little, it's quite difficult for us.

104
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:10,240
It's we can't offer a second, another free trial when you're already a paying subscriber.

105
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:17,760
But what we can do is we can offer you a free taste and then still take you back to the original subscription tier that you were on.

106
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:24,640
And that's been a really effective lever for us to get people to try our highest tier, max, with video calls, especiallyally.

107
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:32,720
One last point is, and when you think about free tastes or free trials, it doesn't just extend to the tiers or the whole product.

108
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:37,040
Sometimes you can even extend it to a single individual feature.

109
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:40,000
We have something called a video call, which is part of the max tier.

110
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:52,880
And what we've tried to, what we've seen sometimes is offering just enough free tastes of that video call just in isolation, just one call, is actually not a bad way to try and get people to try a feature and see if they want to convert.

111
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:54,960
And we've seen that also as an effective lever.

112
00:10:55,360 --> 00:11:02,720
Are there, what are the specific ways you make it really clear that they're getting this reverse trial?

113
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:11,040
is what it's typically called in the industry where you don't have a credit card and they actually have to subscribe to get back to the subscription state.

114
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:12,400
How do you make that clear?

115
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:13,600
Is there a countdown?

116
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:16,240
You know, what kind of copy is on the buttons?

117
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:20,000
Because this is something I know I've heard from other folks who have done this.

118
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:28,880
I've done some of this in my own app that I run on the side and people expect it to auto convert and they're even like scared to like get that free taste.

119
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:31,840
At least like I haven't been able to crack it.

120
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:35,520
I haven't talked to many people who have cracked being able to communicate that effectively.

121
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:36,240
Yeah.

122
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:47,360
Well, the first thing is we usually always have copy that makes it absolutely clear that this is a three day kind of free period or free preview.

123
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:50,720
I think preview is another phrasing that I've used in the industry.

124
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:58,640
The other thing is we actually always position it as like a reward and we say, hey, you just earned, you just earned this.

125
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:07,040
And one of the things that we used to initially have it on our, you know, when you're learning through the course, there's a path, there's a chest on the path in your learning path.

126
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:13,920
And we actually moved it towards after the lesson because it felt more rewarding to earn it after completing a lesson.

127
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:20,960
And so I think when you position it as a reward, it helps like signal that, like, oh, I'm getting this as just like a limited time benefit.

128
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:27,360
The other there are we do use like kind of like some type of timers or concepts of timers to help drive like urgency.

129
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:40,720
When you go to the subscriptions page, you'll also see that you can upgrade any time, but it's super important to like make sure that consumers understand that this is not going to suddenly charge you a different amount.

130
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:56,080
One of the things that of course we always, you know, we talk about clarity, but clarity also comes is super important in just maintaining trust because if suddenly users don't trust what you're doing, then you know, they're will be way more skeptical.

131
00:12:56,240 --> 00:13:04,720
And I think the risk in a product like where you're paying for super and you get a trial for Max is, you know, they may even cancel their super and we of course don't want that.

132
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:12,000
So, and then I would imagine the, the paywall they land on after a free taste looks different.

133
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:15,200
It has different copy than like an onboarding paywall.

134
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:33,440
So does that paywall say like, you know, did you enjoy your three days like extended indefinitely or like are there any kind of copy wins you've had there that kind of help, you know, remind people or or reinforce that like loss aversion, you're going to lose this if you don't like how do you position that after the free taste?

135
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:40,240
Yeah, usually after we we always let users know, I think it's like a home drawer that comes up.

136
00:13:40,560 --> 00:13:44,720
I think one of the things we just emphasize is that your X benefits have ended.

137
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:48,480
And then we usually always give like two sets of options.

138
00:13:49,040 --> 00:13:53,200
One is either to continue or to basically kind of explore and or resubscribe.

139
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:58,480
And I think that the important thing there is that being super clear that it's ended.

140
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:03,680
And two, we're drawing attention to the fact that you can easily rejoin.

141
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:05,280
What about personalization?

142
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:13,360
Are you doing any kind of user level personalization on these paywalls and offers and when to offer the free trials and things like that?

143
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:13,840
Yeah.

144
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:17,520
We've really ramped up, I think, our investments in personalization.

145
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:21,440
optimization and using machine learning to try and optimize a whole host of things.

146
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:36,480
As you know, we of course our freemium or free tier is with ads and those ads are a mix of kind of ads that are promoting our subscription products and also third party ads, promoting websites, mobile, other mobile apps, et cetera.

147
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:49,040
And one of the first few things that we tried starting to optimize was, well, what is the right mix of, you know, promoting our own product versus serving other external ads?

148
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:51,360
And we saw good wins on it.

149
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:56,800
And then that translates also into now, well, okay, now if we know that you're likely to subscribe.

150
00:14:57,280 --> 00:15:00,320
We can probably predict how likely are you to subscribe to which plan.

151
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:13,280
We used to have like fixed ratios on how many ads we show that are for like each tier, but that's quite an inefficient way of operating because we probably have a lot of signals that we can leverage.

152
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:15,840
Did you ever tap on that plan in the past?

153
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:21,680
Did you try to learn more about a video call when we showed you the video call as your next lesson?

154
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:34,880
So for us, we've seen actually like a lot of great wins and it's super important to kind of keep collecting signals that can help you personalize not just basically kind of all aspects of like upsells.

155
00:15:35,520 --> 00:15:38,640
And I think we are still we've just barely scratched the surface of it.

156
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:51,360
And I think there's a lot of like wins that we can go like just getting all the way into, for example, we have many, many creatives that run to promote our super product as an ad at the end of a lesson.

157
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:57,840
And we've had some wins in having a bandit and trying to optimize which creative will work best for who.

158
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:00,800
And we're continuing to see wins from that.

159
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:02,000
Yeah, that's awesome.

160
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:13,520
And always fun to hear that Apple and Duolingo scale is still early in some of the optimizations that we all wish we had time and budget and teams to work on.

161
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:17,360
It's really cool to hear the winds you have seen on personalization in the app.

162
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:32,800
I want to wrap up with something you actually taught me when we were preparing for the podcast, and that is that free trials aren't actually common everywhere around the world and that you do need to take into account the way certain countries are used to buying things.

163
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:34,640
So tell me more about what you learned there.

164
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:39,840
I think free trials are really kind of a not necessarily universal concept.

165
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:52,560
They are getting better understood over time, but there will always be regional differences, whether that's for payment method reasons, regulation reasons or just consumer preferences.

166
00:16:55,520 --> 00:17:00,320
Almost every single subscription service doesn't really offer a free trial.

167
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:03,360
That's absolutely zero dollars to try.

168
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:13,920
They actually prefer a model in which you have to pay a very small nominal amount to get access to a trial, let's say like a dollar or 99 cents.

169
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:19,600
And that's quite interesting because one, the reasons behind this is of course cultural nuances play out.

170
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:23,200
People are not familiar with this auto renewal concept.

171
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:35,760
And two is paid trials is quite interesting because they are actually trying to do something which is similar in what we're talking about about in terms of trying to add clarity that this is a paid product.

172
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:47,680
So, you know, back to my really original point about clarity over persuasion, it's you'll see that like it's so important to make sure that people understand what they're going to be trying or buying.

173
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:56,880
Ultimately, you know, you want to be clear that there will be an annual charge or a monthly charge after your trial that's going to happen on a recurring basis.

174
00:17:57,440 --> 00:18:08,640
And what you see is that in certain markets like China, there are just different preferences and we're still working through those and we want to make sure we can cater to these markets in the right way.

175
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:11,440
China is such an important market and I think paid trials.

176
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:14,800
will probably, you know, it's not just China that I've seen them in.

177
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:16,800
I've seen them in other parts, like in Europe as well.

178
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:18,080
That's so fascinating.

179
00:18:18,240 --> 00:18:24,720
And I mean, I was thinking as you were talking, I mean, it's a weird concept that you're just giving something away, something of value.

180
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:29,840
You don't walk into a restaurant and, like, give me, give me a bite of that steak to see if I want the steak or the fish.

181
00:18:30,400 --> 00:18:35,600
You know, I guess maybe an ice cream counter, like, you can get samples of the different flavors when they have like fifty flavors.

182
00:18:35,840 --> 00:18:40,960
Like, there aren't just, there aren't just, there aren't that many places where you just get something that's truly valuable.

183
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:49,360
It's a weird thing to, like, give something that you're going to charge for that should be truly valuable, but then just give it away, give it, give the free taste away.

184
00:18:49,520 --> 00:18:50,560
So, yeah, I can understand.

185
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:53,600
culturally and just like user psychology.

186
00:18:54,080 --> 00:19:02,560
And I've heard from, you know, especially for folks who are running ads, the signal of a, you know, one dollar thirty day trial kind of thing.

187
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:11,200
It's like the people who are willing to get over that hurdle to a dollar can give you on the day one, much better signal back to the ad networks than running a free trial.

188
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:21,600
So I've even heard paid trials being used for that reason as well, of just getting better signal quicker, because if you're willing to pay a dollar, you're at least willing to pay something for the product.

189
00:19:22,080 --> 00:19:27,360
And it's, you know, so many free trials these days, people just turn them off immediately after starting the free trial.

190
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:29,680
And so it's hard to like get signal from a free trial.

191
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:30,960
That's very true.

192
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:42,480
And I would just say like the day zero cancellation is such an important, like, kind of thing to track if you're ever running a free trial because it's a good indicator of whether like your users really have high intent or not.

193
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:45,760
Once they've cancelled, it's really hard to get them to cancel.

194
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:58,640
And the other thing is that like, I think you have to realize that if you don't earn trust, then it's going to translate into either just poor conversions or even customer complaints that like, oh, I didn't expect this charge.

195
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:00,400
So super important to get it right.

